A \textit{crossed system} of groups is a quadruple $(H, G, \alpha,
f)$, where $H$ and $G$ are two groups, $\alpha : G \rightarrow
{\rm Aut}\, (H)$ and $f : G \times G \rightarrow H$ are two maps such
that the following compatibility conditions hold:
\begin{eqnarray}
g_1 \triangleright (g_2\triangleright h) &=& f(g_1, g_2) \,
\bigl((g_1g_2)\triangleright  h \bigl)\, f(g_1, g_2)^{-1}
\eqlabel{WA} \\
f(g_1,\, g_2)\, f(g_1 g_2, \, g_3) &=&  \bigl(g_1 \triangleright
f(g_2, \, g_3) \bigl) \, f(g_1, \, g_2g_3) \eqlabel{CC}
\end{eqnarray}
for all $g_1$, $g_2$, $g_3 \in G$ and $h\in H$.

